<article class="disney"><header><div class="meta case-meta"><span class="case-meta__client-full">Walt Disney World</span><span class="case-meta__client-short">Disney</span><span class="case-meta__highlight">−44% exit rate</span><span class="case-meta__role">Senior UX Strategist</span></div><div class="intro"><div><p>Research-Led UX &amp; Information Architecture</p><h1>Public Affairs<br /><span class="heading-accent">Digital</span><br />Transformation</h1></div><p>Public affairs information architecture redesign that made Disney's community impact finally visible. Restructured navigation for three distinct user journeys, cut site-wide exit rates by 44%, and streamlined donation flow from 5+ steps to 2 interactions.</p></div></header>
  <CaseContents links={[{"href": "#case-section-1", "label": "Discovery"}, {"href": "#case-section-2", "label": "Information architecture"}, {"href": "#case-section-3", "label": "Delivery"}, {"href": "#case-section-4", "label": "Results"}]} /><figure class="hero"><img src="/images/disney_hero.webp" alt="Cinderella Castle at Walt Disney World" loading="eager" decoding="async" /></figure>
<section class="research" id="case-section-1" tabindex="-1"><div><p>01 / Discovery</p><h2>Three journeys.<br />One hidden service.</h2></div><div class="copy"><p>Competitive audit across 12 Fortune 500 Public Affairs sites and theme park properties. Heatmap analysis identified navigation invisibility issues. Moderated sessions with 8 participants showed users couldn't distinguish Public Affairs content from the main resort site.</p><p>This wasn't a copy or visual design problem—it was a fundamental positioning failure. Users arriving to donate or find media information couldn't tell they were in the right place. No amount of optimization could fix architecture-level confusion.</p></div><div class="research__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney01.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs desktop design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney02.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs mobile design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney03-1.webp" alt="Disney Public Affairs wireframe exploration" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div></section>
<section class="architecture" id="case-section-2" tabindex="-1"><div class="architecture__head"><p>02 / Information architecture</p><h2>Make the task<br /><span class="heading-accent">the entry point.</span></h2></div><div class="copy"><p>Restructured hierarchy for three distinct user journeys: media professionals, community organizations, and general stakeholders. Each audience received a dedicated entry point with content organized around their specific tasks rather than Disney's internal communication structure.</p><p>Navigation language was replaced with user-facing task descriptions. Press professionals found press releases organized by topic and date. Community organizations found donation information prominently at the top level. General stakeholders found company impact stories and community programs.</p></div><figure><img src="/images/disney04.webp" alt="Disney information architecture wireframes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></section>
<section class="implementation" id="case-section-3" tabindex="-1"><div><p>03 / Responsive delivery</p><h2>Flow Optimization &amp;<br />Visual Design</h2></div><div class="implementation__images"><figure><img src="/images/disney05-1.webp" alt="Disney mobile navigation design" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure><figure><img src="/images/disney07.webp" alt="Disney responsive UI system" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></figure></div><div class="copy"><p>Donation flow was reduced from 5+ steps to 2 interactions with 5/5 task success in testing. Press content was separated into categorized feeds by topic and audience type, eliminating the single undifferentiated stream that had caused journalist abandonment.</p><p>Visual design established clear distinction between Disney Public Affairs and the main resort brand while maintaining the organizational credibility required for media and community audiences. Fully responsive implementation across all breakpoints replaced a desktop-only experience that had failed mobile visitors entirely.</p></div></section>
<section class="results" id="case-section-4" tabindex="-1"><div><p>04 / Results</p><h2>Results &amp;<br />Impact Metrics</h2></div><div class="numbers"><div><b>44%</b><span>decrease in site-wide exit rate</span></div><div><b>24%</b><span>decrease in category-page bounce rate</span></div><div><b>26%</b><span>increase in session duration</span></div></div><div class="copy"><p>44% decrease in site-wide exit rate. 24% decrease in bounce rate across category pages. 26% increase in session duration. These improvements came directly from fixing the information architecture—users could finally find what they came for.</p><p>Donation flow success was now 5/5 in testing where it had previously been buried and invisible. Mobile usability improved from non-functional to fully operational across all breakpoints. The platform now properly represented Disney's commitment to community engagement.</p></div></section></article>
